\begin{proof}
Let $G$ be a group of order $5$. Since $|G|>1$, choose $g\in G\setminus\{e\}$. Lagrange's theorem implies that $\operatorname{ord}(g)$ divides $5$. The only divisors of $5$ are $1$ and $5$, and $\operatorname{ord}(g)\neq 1$ because $g\neq e$. Hence $\operatorname{ord}(g)=5$.

The subgroup $\langle g\rangle$ therefore has $5$ elements, so $\langle g\rangle=G$. Thus $G$ is cyclic. Every cyclic group is abelian, since powers of its generator commute. Consequently, $G$ is abelian.
\end{proof}
